.TH MHFWUPDATER 1 "November 2026" "mhfwupdater" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
mhfwupdater \- firmware updater for microHAM USB devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mhfwupdater
.RB [ \-v | \-vv ]
.B list
.br
.B mhfwupdater
.RB [ \-v | \-vv ]
.B info
.IR file .\|.\|.
.br
.B mhfwupdater
.RB [ \-v | \-vv ]
.B probe
.RB [ \-d
.IR device ]
.RB [ \-\-bootloader ]
.br
.B mhfwupdater
.RB [ \-v | \-vv ]
.B flash
.RB [ \-d
.IR device ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-invalidate ]
.RB [ \-\-recover ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mhfwupdater
writes the firmware files
.RI ( *.cbl )
distributed by microHAM to microHAM USB devices, which microHAM Router does
on Windows.
.PP
Before anything is written, the firmware file is checked against the device:
the product type must match and the device's hardware revision must meet the
firmware's requirements.
Downgrades and rewriting the installed version are allowed.
.PP
Devices are found through sysfs by their USB ID 0403:eeef.
The stock
.B ftdi_sio
kernel driver makes them available as
.IR /dev/ttyUSB n,
which needs read and write access, usually through membership in the
.B dialout
group.
.PP
.BR mhuxd (8)
opens the device ports exclusively, so stop it before an update
.RB ( "systemctl stop mhuxd" )
and start it again afterwards.
This takes all devices it serves offline in the meantime.
The device restarts during the update.
Don't transmit while updating.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
List the attached microHAM devices: tty, serial number and product name.
.TP
.BI info " file" .\|.\|.
Show the contents of firmware files: device, version, hardware requirements
and size.
.TP
.B probe
Show the firmware version of a device.
With
.BR \-\-bootloader ,
the device also restarts into its bootloader to report the bootloader version,
then returns to its firmware.
Nothing is written.
.TP
.BI flash " file"
Write a firmware file to a device.
Shows the installed and the new firmware and asks for confirmation before
writing.
.TP
.B help
Print the help, or the help of the given command.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print debug messages.
Given twice
.RB ( \-vv ),
also print all bytes sent and received.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help.
After a command, print the help of that command.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SS probe and flash
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-device " \fIdevice\fR"
The device to use, by serial number (as shown by
.BR list )
or path
.RI ( /dev/ttyUSB1 ).
Needed only when several devices are attached.
The serial number is the better choice, because ttyUSB numbers can change
when devices are replugged.
.SS probe
.TP
.B \-\-bootloader
Also restart into the bootloader and read its version.
.SS flash
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Check the file against the device and enter the bootloader, but write
nothing.
The bootloader times out and restarts the installed firmware.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-invalidate
Don't write the placeholder start page that protects against interrupted
updates (see
.BR "INTERRUPTED UPDATES" ).
.TP
.B \-\-recover
Don't ask the firmware to restart, wait up to 60 seconds for the bootloader
instead.
Use this to finish an interrupted update, and power-cycle the device when
asked.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Don't ask for confirmation.
Needed when standard input is not a terminal.
.SH SUPPORTED DEVICES
.TP 12
.B mmk_*.cbl
micro KEYER, CW KEYER, DIGI KEYER
.TP
.B mpk_*.cbl
micro KEYER II
.TP
.B mk3_*.cbl
micro KEYER III
.TP
.B dk2_*.cbl
DIGI KEYER II
.TP
.B mok_*.cbl
micro KEYER 2R, 2R+
.TP
.B u2r_*.cbl
micro 2R
.TP
.B sml_*.cbl
Station Master
.TP
.B smd_*.cbl
Station Master DeLuxe
.PP
Tested on the micro KEYER III and the DIGI KEYER II.
.SH INTERRUPTED UPDATES
If an update is interrupted (power loss, USB unplugged, program killed), the
device is left with incomplete firmware and no longer answers
.BR probe .
The bootloader still starts at every power-up, so the update can be finished
with
.B flash \-\-recover
and the same file.
Power-cycle the device when asked.
.PP
To keep an interrupted update from starting half-written firmware,
.B flash
first writes a placeholder start page that jumps straight back into the
bootloader, and writes the real start page last.
A device interrupted this way stays in its bootloader.
The micro KEYER III then shows "Uploading Firmware", but its bootloader only
works properly again after a power cycle: switch it off completely (USB and
power supply), start
.B flash \-\-recover
and switch it on.
.PP
If a device rejects the placeholder page, nothing is written and the update
continues without the protection.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error, reported on standard error.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line, or no command given.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B cannot open /dev/ttyUSB0 (is mhuxd running?)
.BR mhuxd (8)
or another program has the port open, or the user lacks access to it.
.TP
.B no answer from the device firmware
The device is switched off, or its firmware is incomplete: use
.BR \-\-recover .
.TP
.B bootloader did not answer
Try again.
If it keeps failing, run with
.B \-vv
to see the traffic.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the devices, check a firmware file and update the device:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mhfwupdater list
/dev/ttyUSB0  M32VFFGJ  micro KEYER III
$ mhfwupdater info mk3_release_02_12.cbl
$ mhfwupdater flash mk3_release_02_12.cbl
.fi
.RE
.PP
Finish an interrupted update of a particular device:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mhfwupdater flash \-\-recover \-d M32VFFGJ mk3_release_02_12.cbl
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mhuxd (8)
.PP
